  "mechanismSummary": "Class I aldolases use a lysine to turn a ketone into an enamine. Dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP) is first bound to lysine as a protonated imine (formed just as acetone and methylamine form an imine). Removing the C–H next to the C=N⁺ gives an enamine, which adds to the aldehyde of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate: an aldol reaction. Hydrolysis of the iminium ion then releases fructose 1,6-bisphosphate and frees the lysine. Models: methylamine for lysine and acetate for the active-site acid and base groups (in the enzyme several residues share the proton transfers); the phosphate groups take no part and are drawn as PO₃H₂ (dianions at pH 7).",
